-- Point-in-time DATA snapshot of the public schema.
-- Project: ffymjxjcnwakgdmldpne (hosted).  Captured: 2026-08-22.
--
-- WHAT THIS IS
-- One plain, read-only copy of every populated table in the public schema,
-- taken with `supabase db dump --linked --data-only --schema public`. It exists
-- so this data survives outside the remote database.
--
-- COVERAGE: 17 tables, 1371 rows, verified against the live remote at capture
-- time. Counts were confirmed two ways -- read from the remote over PostgREST
-- with an exact count, and re-parsed out of this file itself:
--
--     court_form_library        723      legal_issue_rules          28
--     court_form_fields         136      legal_question_rules       10
--     court_forms               113      civil_form_lookup           8
--     forms                     104      legal_evidence_rules        8
--     legal_procedure_rules      55      legal_risk_rules            8
--     form_rules                 50      legal_element_rules         7
--     pdf_form_inventory         50      court_form_sources          6
--     small_claims_form_lookup   32      pdf_overlay_fields          2
--     legal_form_mapping_rules   31
--
-- The remaining 7 tables in the schema were empty at capture time and are
-- therefore absent: cases, case_documents, case_evidence,
-- case_generated_documents, case_intakes, pdf_field_mappings,
-- court_form_overlays.
--
-- NO USER DATA
-- Everything here is reference/catalogue data. The user-data tables (cases and
-- the case_* family) were verified empty and are excluded. They hold PII and
-- must not be snapshotted into version control -- if they ever hold rows, this
-- file is the wrong mechanism for them.
--
-- WHAT THIS IS NOT
-- It is NOT wired into seed.sql, NOT run by `supabase db reset`, and NOT
-- automated. Nothing loads it unless a human does. It is a snapshot, not a
-- backup system, and it is stale the moment data changes on the remote. It does
-- not replace Supabase's own backups.
--
-- RESTORING
-- The INSERTs are plain and carry no ON CONFLICT clause, so they load cleanly
-- only into EMPTY tables. Against tables that already hold rows they fail on
-- duplicate keys -- deliberately, so a restore cannot silently half-apply. To
-- reload intentionally, TRUNCATE the target tables first.
--
-- None of these 17 tables has a foreign key (all 9 in the schema belong to the
-- case_* family), so load order does not matter.
--
-- The setval() calls at the end restore identity high-water marks for the 10
-- tables that use identity columns. Without them the next INSERT into those
-- tables would collide. The other 7 tables key on uuid and have no sequence.
-- Every setval present in the dump is carried through, rather than assuming a
-- "<table>_id_seq" naming convention.

SET statement_timeout = 0;
SET client_encoding = 'UTF8';
SET standard_conforming_strings = on;
SELECT pg_catalog.set_config('search_path', '', false);

